{"name":"Boreal","hex":"#717e73","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Boreal","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A subdivision of the Holocene epoch."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Boreal"}],"entries":[{"title":"Boreal period","blocks":[{"text":"In paleoclimatology of the Holocene, the Boreal was the first of the Blytt–Sernander sequence of north European climatic phases that were originally based on the study of Danish peat bogs, named for Axel Blytt and Rutger Sernander, who first established the sequence. In peat bog sediments, the Boreal is also recognized by its characteristic pollen zone. It was preceded by the Younger Dryas, the last cold snap of the Pleistocene, and followed by the Atlantic, a warmer and moister period than our most recent climate. The Boreal, transitional between the two periods, varied a great deal, at times having within it climates like today's."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Utro_v_sosnovom_lesu.jpg/330px-Utro_v_sosnovom_lesu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boreal_period","wikidata":"Q893477"},{"title":"Subarctic climate","blocks":[{"text":"The subarctic climate is a continental climate with long, cold winters, and short, warm to cool summers. It is found on large landmasses, often away from the moderating effects of an ocean, generally at latitudes from 50°N to 70°N, poleward of the humid continental climates. Like other Class D climates, they are rare in the Southern Hemisphere, only found at some isolated highland elevations. Subarctic or boreal climates are the source regions for the cold air that affects temperate latitudes to the south in winter. These climates represent Köppen climate classification Dfc, Dwc, Dsc, Dfd, Dwd and Dsd."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Koppen_subarctic_world_map_1991-2020_-3C_borderless.png/330px-Koppen_subarctic_world_map_1991-2020_-3C_borderless.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Subarctic_climate","wikidata":"Q5967371"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Pine_tree_rings.JPG/500px-Pine_tree_rings.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Pine_tree_rings.JPG/1280px-Pine_tree_rings.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Pine_tree_rings.JPG/500px-Pine_tree_rings.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pine tree rings","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APine_tree_rings.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Varve1.gif/250px-Varve1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/39/Varve1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Varve1.gif/250px-Varve1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Varves","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVarve1.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Dds40-097_large.jpeg/500px-Dds40-097_large.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Dds40-097_large.jpeg/500px-Dds40-097_large.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Distribution of tephra","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADds40-097_large.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Peat_artisanal_exploitation2.jpg/500px-Peat_artisanal_exploit